### What is tree diameter counted in nodes?

The diameter is the number of nodes on the longest path between any two nodes. This differs from the edge-counted version by exactly one. The path can start and end anywhere and does not need to pass through the root.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class DeskNode {
    public String deskId;
    public DeskNode left;
    public DeskNode right;

    public DeskNode(String deskId) {
        this.deskId = deskId;
    }
}
```

### How does the efficient post-order solution work?

A helper returns the height of a subtree. Before returning, it updates a shared maximum with `leftHeight + rightHeight + 1`, which is the candidate diameter through this node counted in nodes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.DeskNode;

public class TreeDiameterNodes {
    private int maxDiameter = 0;

    public int diameter(DeskNode root) {
        maxDiameter = 0;
        height(root);
        return maxDiameter;
    }

    private int height(DeskNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        int left = height(node.left);
        int right = height(node.right);

        // Candidate diameter in nodes
        maxDiameter = Math.max(maxDiameter, left + right + 1);

        return 1 + Math.max(left, right);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //       CEO
        //      /   \
        //    VP1   VP2
        //   /       \
        //  DIR1     DIR2
        //  /
        // MGR1
        DeskNode ceo = new DeskNode("CEO");
        DeskNode vp1 = new DeskNode("VP1");
        DeskNode vp2 = new DeskNode("VP2");
        DeskNode dir1 = new DeskNode("DIR1");
        DeskNode dir2 = new DeskNode("DIR2");
        DeskNode mgr1 = new DeskNode("MGR1");

        ceo.left = vp1; ceo.right = vp2;
        vp1.left = dir1; vp2.right = dir2;
        dir1.left = mgr1;

        TreeDiameterNodes td = new TreeDiameterNodes();
        System.out.println("Diameter (nodes): " + td.diameter(ceo));
    }
}
```

```text
Diameter (nodes): 6
```

### What is the difference between node-counted and edge-counted diameter?

Node-counted: `leftHeight + rightHeight + 1`. Edge-counted: `leftHeight + rightHeight`. A single node has diameter 1 (nodes) or 0 (edges). Always state which convention you are using.

| Convention | Formula | Single node | Path A-B-C |
|---|---|---|---|
| Nodes | left + right + 1 | 1 | 3 |
| Edges | left + right | 0 | 2 |

```java
// Nodes version
maxDiameter = Math.max(maxDiameter, left + right + 1);

// Edges version
maxDiameter = Math.max(maxDiameter, left + right);
```

### Why is the naive approach quadratic?

The naive approach calls a separate height function at every node. On a skewed tree, height at the root is O(n), at its child O(n-1), totaling O(n^2). The fused approach computes each node's height once.

```java
// NAIVE: O(n^2) on skewed trees
public int naiveDiameter(DeskNode root) {
    if (root == null) return 0;
    int through = heightSeparate(root.left) + heightSeparate(root.right) + 1;
    return Math.max(through, Math.max(naiveDiameter(root.left), naiveDiameter(root.right)));
}
```

### Why can the longest path skip the root?

The longest path connects two nodes through their lowest common ancestor, which may be any node in the tree. Evaluating the candidate at every node ensures paths not passing through the root are captured.

```java
//     A
//    /
//   B
//  / \
// C   D
//      \
//       E
// Longest: C → B → D → E = 4 nodes, goes through B not A
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty tree (diameter 0), single node (diameter 1), and a completely skewed tree.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.DeskNode;

public class DiameterNodesEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeDiameterNodes td = new TreeDiameterNodes();
        System.out.println("Empty: " + td.diameter(null));
        System.out.println("Single: " + td.diameter(new DeskNode("A")));

        DeskNode skewed = new DeskNode("A");
        skewed.left = new DeskNode("B");
        skewed.left.left = new DeskNode("C");
        System.out.println("Skewed: " + td.diameter(skewed));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: 0
Single: 1
Skewed: 3
```

### How is this related to the edge-counted version and maximum path sum?

All three problems share the same post-order pattern: return one value to the parent while updating a global with another. In diameter (nodes): return height, update `left + right + 1`. In diameter (edges): return height, update `left + right`. In maximum path sum: return upward gain, update bending path sum.

```java
// Shared pattern
private int helper(Node node) {
    if (node == null) return 0;
    int left = helper(node.left);
    int right = helper(node.right);
    global = Math.max(global, COMBINE(left, right, node));
    return PROPAGATE(left, right, node);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer states the counting convention explicitly and derives the O(n) solution by pointing to the repeated work in the naive approach. The fused post-order pattern is a reusable tool across multiple tree problems.

In production at National Bank of India, this analysis measures the longest escalation chain in the org hierarchy. An over-long chain slows approvals, so the diameter is reported as a health metric.

```java
int chainLength = new TreeDiameterNodes().diameter(orgRoot);
if (chainLength > THRESHOLD) {
    alertService.send("Escalation chain " + chainLength + " nodes exceeds limit.");
}
```
